<body>
<div class="container">
<header>
    <h1>Global Crude Oil Price Trends</h1>
    <p>A Historical Analysis (1970–2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Monthly Time-Series Trend Report for Energy Analysts</p>
</header>

<!-- Executive Overview -->
<div class="section" id="executive-overview">
    <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
    <p>This report examines <strong>{stats['n_rows']:,} monthly observations</strong> of global crude oil prices from <strong>{stats['start_date']}</strong> through <strong>{stats['end_date']}</strong>. Over this {stats['years_span']:.0f}-year span, nominal prices rose from <strong>${stats['first_price']:.2f}/bbl</strong> to <strong>${stats['last_price']:.2f}/bbl</strong>, yielding a compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of <strong>{stats['cagr_pct']:.2f}%</strong>. The dataset captures six distinct pricing regimes — from the pre-crisis stability of the early 1970s, through the inflationary shocks of the late 1970s, the 1986 collapse, the 1990s doldrums, the China-driven super-cycle of the 2000s, the shale volatility of the 2010s, and the COVID-19 and geopolitical disruptions of the 2020s.</p>
    <div class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Overall Price Range</div><div class="value">${stats['price_range']:.2f}</div><div class="sub">Min: ${stats['min_price']:.2f} &nbsp; Max: ${stats['max_price']:.2f}</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="label">CAGR</div><div class="value">{stats['cagr_pct']:.2f}%</div><div class="sub">Over {stats['years_span']:.1f} years</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Max MoM Change</div><div class="value">{stats['max_mom_pct']:.1f}%</div><div class="sub">{stats['max_mom_date']} (${stats['max_mom_prev']:.2f} → ${stats['max_mom_val']:.2f})</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Max YoY Change</div><div class="value">{stats['max_yoy_pct']:.1f}%</div><div class="sub">{stats['max_yoy_date']} (${stats['max_yoy_prev']:.2f} → ${stats['max_yoy_val']:.2f})</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Highest Avg Decade</div><div class="value">{stats['highest_avg_decade']}s</div><div class="sub">Mean: ${[d for d in stats['decade_stats'] if d['Decade']==stats['highest_avg_decade']][0]['mean']:.2f}/bbl</div></div>
        <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Price Volatility (Std Dev)</div><div class="value">${stats['std_price']:.2f}</div><div class="sub">Long-term dispersion</div></div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Long-Term Trend -->
<div class="section" id="long-term-trend">
    <h2>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
    <p>The full time series reveals three super-cycles: (1) the 1970s structural break from sub-$3 to $40/bbl, (2) the 2000s demand boom that pushed prices from ~$25 to an all-time nominal high of <span class="highlight">${stats['max_price']:.2f}</span> in July 2008, and (3) the post-2010 oscillation between shale-driven oversupply and OPEC+ restraint. The chart below annotates the most consequential inflection points.</p>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['long_term_trend']}" alt="Long-term trend"></div>
    <p><strong>Key observations:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>The 1973–74 embargo produced the largest single-month jump on record: <strong>+{stats['max_mom_pct']:.1f}%</strong> in January 1974 ($4.10 → $13.00).</li>
        <li>The 2008 peak ($132.83) was followed by a historic four-month collapse of <strong>−69%</strong> to $41.34 by December 2008.</li>
        <li>The 2020 COVID-19 shock saw prices fall <strong>−39.6% MoM</strong> in March and <strong>−34.7%</strong> in April, bottoming at $21.04 — the lowest since the early 2000s.</li>
        <li>The 2022 Russia-Ukraine conflict drove a <strong>+20.2% MoM</strong> spike in March 2022, lifting prices above $112/bbl.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- Decade-by-Decade -->
<div class="section" id="decade-by-decade">
    <h2>Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
    <p>Comparing decades exposes stark differences in pricing environments. The 2010s recorded the highest mean price ($76.45/bbl), while the 1990s were the most stable (coefficient of variation = 0.21). The 2000s combined the highest peak with the greatest intra-decade volatility (CV = 0.52).</p>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['decade_comparison']}" alt="Decade comparison"></div>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Mean Price</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>Min</th><th>Max</th><th>Coeff. of Variation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {decade_rows}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <div style="margin-top:18px;">
        <p><strong>1970s:</strong> {stats['decade_events']['1970']}</p>
        <p><strong>1980s:</strong> {stats['decade_events']['1980']}</p>
        <p><strong>1990s:</strong> {stats['decade_events']['1990']}</p>
        <p><strong>2000s:</strong> {stats['decade_events']['2000']}</p>
        <p><strong>2010s:</strong> {stats['decade_events']['2010']}</p>
        <p><strong>2020s:</strong> {stats['decade_events']['2020']}</p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Volatility and Shocks -->
<div class="section" id="volatility-and-shocks">
    <h2>Price Volatility & Major Shocks</h2>
    <p>Volatility is not evenly distributed. The rolling 12-month standard deviation of month-over-month percentage changes spikes during crises and compresses during stable-demand periods. The 1970s, 2008–09, 2014–16, and 2020–22 stand out as high-volatility regimes.</p>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['volatility_rolling']}" alt="Rolling volatility"></div>
    <div class="two-col">
        <div>
            <h3 style="color:var(--primary);margin-top:0;">Largest Single-Month Moves</h3>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Price</th><th>MoM</th><th>YoY</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    {shock_rows}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3 style="color:var(--primary);margin-top:0;">Shock Context</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>1973–74:</strong> OPEC embargo and supply cuts. Prices quadrupled in months.</li>
                <li><strong>1979:</strong> Iranian Revolution removed ~5% of global supply.</li>
                <li><strong>1986:</strong> Saudi Arabia shifted to netback pricing; prices collapsed 67% from Nov 1985 to July 1986.</li>
                <li><strong>1990–91:</strong> Gulf War spike was brief; prices normalized within six months.</li>
                <li><strong>2008:</strong> Financial crisis erased demand expectations; speculative unwinding accelerated the drop.</li>
                <li><strong>2014–16:</strong> Shale oversupply and OPEC market-share strategy pushed Brent below $30.</li>
                <li><strong>2020:</strong> COVID-19 lockdowns caused an unprecedented demand shock (−20% global consumption).</li>
                <li><strong>2022:</strong> Sanctions on Russian exports tightened supply amid recovering demand.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['shocks_scatter']}" alt="Shocks scatter"></div>
</div>

<!-- Statistical Summary -->
<div class="section" id="statistical-summary">
    <h2>Statistical Summary & Distribution</h2>
    <p>The distribution of monthly prices is heavily right-skewed: the median ($29.11) sits well below the mean ($39.80), reflecting the long tail of high-price episodes. The inter-quartile range ($16.53–$61.25) captures where prices have spent most of their time.</p>
    <div class="two-col">
        <div>
            <div class="kpi-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;">
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Mean</div><div class="value">${stats['mean_price']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Median</div><div class="value">${stats['median_price']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Std Dev</div><div class="value">${stats['std_price']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Min</div><div class="value">${stats['min_price']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Max</div><div class="value">${stats['max_price']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Range</div><div class="value">${stats['price_range']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">10th Percentile</div><div class="value">${stats['p10']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">25th Percentile</div><div class="value">${stats['p25']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">75th Percentile</div><div class="value">${stats['p75']:.2f}</div></div>
                <div class="kpi"><div class="label">90th Percentile</div><div class="value">${stats['p90']:.2f}</div></div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div>
            <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['distribution']}" alt="Distribution"></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Outlook and Conclusions -->
<div class="section" id="outlook-and-conclusions">
    <h2>Outlook & Conclusions</h2>
    <p><strong>Key takeaways:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Crude oil prices exhibit <strong>regime-dependent behavior</strong>: long periods of stability or gradual drift are punctuated by sharp, discontinuous shocks driven by geopolitics, financial crises, or technological disruption.</li>
        <li>The <strong>2010s remain the most expensive decade on average</strong> ($76.45/bbl), but the 2020s are close behind ($71.75/bbl through early 2026), suggesting a structurally higher floor than the 1990s–2000s.</li>
        <li><strong>Volatility clustering</strong> is evident: high-volatility episodes (1973–74, 2008–09, 2020–22) are followed by elevated uncertainty for 12–24 months before mean-reversion in volatility.</li>
        <li>The <strong>CAGR of 7.93%</strong> over 56 years far exceeds broad inflation, indicating that crude oil has delivered a positive real return as a scarce energy input — but with extreme drawdown risk.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>2020s trajectory:</strong> After the COVID-19 collapse to $21.04 (April 2020), prices rebounded to >$110 in 2022, then moderated toward $60–$80 by late 2024–early 2025. The March 2026 observation ($87.84) hints at renewed supply tightness or demand resilience, though analysts should treat the most recent months as potentially estimated or projected values.</p>
    <p><strong>For analysts:</strong> When modeling future prices, the historical record suggests anchoring on decade-specific means and volatilities rather than a single long-run average. The right-skewed distribution and fat tails (evident in the shock table) imply that tail-risk hedging remains essential for energy portfolios.</p>
</div>

<div class="footer">
    Report generated from data.csv ({stats['n_rows']:,} monthly observations) &nbsp;|&nbsp; All statistics computed directly from the dataset. No inflation adjustment applied.
</div>
</div>
</body>
</html>'''

